          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is cognitive behavioural therapy described as first-line for insomnia rather than medication?",
          "answer": "Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Insomnia (CBT-I) is recommended as the first-line treatment for insomnia primarily due to its long-lasting benefits and safety compared to medications. According to guidance from sleep medicine and internal medicine authorities, CBT-I is preferred because:\n\n1. **Durability of Effects**: The positive effects of CBT-I tend to persist even after the treatment has ended, whereas the effects of medications generally diminish quickly once they are stopped (from \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment\").\n\n2. **Avoidance of Long-term Risks**: CBT-I reduces reliance on hypnotics, which can have risks associated with long-term use, such as dependency or tolerance (from \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment\").\n\n3. **Effective Components**: CBT-I incorporates structured techniques that help individuals develop better sleep habits and manage their sleep environment. It typically includes stimulus control and sleep restriction strategies, among other components, delivered over several sessions (from \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment\").\n\nEvidence supporting the effectiveness of CBT-I over medications includes systematic reviews and clinical studies indicating that CBT-I can lead to improvements in sleep quality and overall well-being without the side effects commonly associated with pharmacological treatments (from \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment\" and \"Insomnia\"). \n\nIn contrast, medications may offer short-term symptom relief but often do not address underlying sleep issues and can lead to other adverse effects (from \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment\"). Therefore, CBT-I is viewed as a more sustainable, effective, and safer approach to treating insomnia.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
